# Vauxhall 20-60 saloon, 1927-1930.
#
# The 20-60: announced on 28 September 1927, the R-type 2,762 cc six joined
# by the T-type 2,916 cc from 1928. The name covers two engine variants of one
# chassis and body family, not two shells, so one object represents them. In
# October 1930 it was renamed the Eighty, which is a different, larger car and
# is not represented.
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vauxhall_20-60
#
# Dimensions: wheelbase 123 in (3.124 m) or 130 in (3.302 m), track 56 in.
# Length, width and height were not established from a source and are
# estimated from the photograph; the estimate is recorded.
#
# Shape reference: NO true level profile exists. Image 1 is about 30 degrees
# off the side, so the flank is estimated within the published wheelbase and
# the limitation is recorded.
#
# Colours: grade (d); no Vauxhall colour list was found. Black, Maroon, Cream
# and Dark Blue are observed colours. Weights 12, 10, 9, 8, total 39,
# provisional.
#
# Speed 110 km/h. Comfort 30/375, on the line 330 + 1.5 x 30. Classes 2,3,4.
# Dates: intro October 1927; retires staggered 1947-1950 as a road tail.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/vauxhall-20-60.blend. Interchange:
# citycars/4.5/vauxhall-20-60.dae. References:
# citycars/references/vauxhall-20-60/ (gitignored; Wikimedia Commons).
#
# Accepted deviations: the saloon is modelled on the STANDARD 3.124 m chassis,
# not the 3.302 m long chassis. The overall dimensions are estimates. The
# survivor's whitewall tyres and wire wheels are simplified to dark tyres and
# low-poly hubs.


obj=citycar
name=Vauxhall2060(Black)
copyright=JamesPetts

DistributionWeight=12
intro_year=1927
intro_month=10
retire_year=1950
retire_month=6
